{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Christopher "Chris" Tarbell is a former FBI special agent who infiltrated both Anonymous and the Silk Road.","title":"Chris Tarbell"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" He was involved in the tracking and arrest of both Sabu (of Anonymous) and Dread Pirate Roberts of Silk Road (Ross Ulbricht).","title":"Chris Tarbell"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" He turned Sabu into an informant against the hacker group Lulzsec.","title":"Chris Tarbell"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" He later led the team that led to the arrest of Ross Ulbricht in the Glen Park Public Library.","title":"Chris Tarbell"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Bartholomew Roberts (17 May 1682 – 10 February 1722), born John Roberts, was a Welsh pirate who raided ships off the Americas and West Africa between 1719 and 1722.","title":"Bartholomew Roberts"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" He was the most successful pirate of the Golden Age of Piracy as measured by vessels captured, taking over 400 prizes in his career.","title":"Bartholomew Roberts"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" He is also known as Black Bart (Welsh: "Barti Ddu" ), but this name was never used in his lifetime.","title":"Bartholomew Roberts"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" He is sometimes confused with Charles Bolles, called the "Black Bart" of the American West.","title":"Bartholomew Roberts"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Ross William Ulbricht (born March 27, 1984) is a former darknet market operator, best known for being convicted of creating and running the Silk Road website until his arrest.","title":"Ross Ulbricht"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" He was known under the pseudonym "Dread Pirate Roberts".","title":"Ross Ulbricht"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Christopher Moore was a co-founder of the offshore pirate radio ship Radio Caroline, and the first voice to be heard on the air from that station.","title":"Christopher Moore (DJ)"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" His opening words were "This is Radio Caroline on 199, your all-day music station".","title":"Christopher Moore (DJ)"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The first song played was by The Rolling Stones.","title":"Christopher Moore (DJ)"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" At its peak in 1967, the station had 23 million listeners, and it revolutionized radio broadcasting in the UK.","title":"Christopher Moore (DJ)"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" In 1991 Moore was interviewed extensively in the BBC TV show A Pirate's Tale, where he described his key role in detail.","title":"Christopher Moore (DJ)"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" Moore is a member of the Pirate Radio Hall of Fame.","title":"Christopher Moore (DJ)"},{"sentence_id":6,"text":" Moore, who had variously been a club DJ, merchant naval steward, and photographer had become involved in Radio Caroline when he met the station's founder Ronan O'Rahilly.","title":"Christopher Moore (DJ)"},{"sentence_id":7,"text":" Moore's Chelsea flat mate Ian Ross (later a novelist) introduced O'Rahilly to his father New Zealand born Charles Ross, who in turn helped O'Rahilly raise the £250,000 needed to start what became Britain's first pirate radio station in April 1964.","title":"Christopher Moore (DJ)"},{"sentence_id":8,"text":" <> http://www.flashesandflames.com/2014/03/how-a-radio-ship-and-7-men-shook-up-britain-50-years-ago/</.>","title":"Christopher Moore (DJ)"},{"sentence_id":9,"text":" Although Chris Moore was the first voice to be heard on Radio Caroline, the first programme was hosted by Simon Dee who subsequently became a TV chat show host of Dee Time on the BBC.","title":"Christopher Moore (DJ)"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Dread Pirate Roberts is a fictional character in the novel "The Princess Bride" (1973) and its 1987 film adaptation.","title":"Dread Pirate Roberts"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Suds Merrick (died 1884) was a New York river pirate and member of the Hook Gang during the 1870s.","title":"Suds Merrick"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Merrick, along with Tommy Shay, James Coffee, and Terry Le Strange, operated in the New York waterfront and involved in burglary and pickpocketing as well as raiding the nearby vessels anchored in the Hudson River.","title":"Suds Merrick"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Merrick would co-lead the gang with Le Strange during the early 1870s until the arrest of Sam McCracken, Tommy Bonner, and Johnny Gallagher after they had looted the canal boat "Thomas H. Brick" and sent to Auburn State Prison in 1874.","title":"Suds Merrick"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Following this incident, Merrick turned control of the Hookers to Bum Mahoney although he would remain with the gang in a limited capacity until his death in 1884.","title":"Suds Merrick"}],"peer_messages":["Unverified peer answer: Christopher "Chris" Tarbell
